WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.687
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.987 --> 00:00:09.060
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:19.117
Unit fifty-seven: Wisdom Traditions. In this unit, you will compare ancient wisdom traditions in a well-organised essay.

4
00:00:20.117 --> 00:00:21.199
Stoicism.

5
00:00:21.899 --> 00:00:28.264
Stoicism means an ancient Greek and Roman philosophy teaching calm acceptance of what we cannot control.

6
00:00:28.564 --> 00:00:32.293
Stoicism teaches us to focus only on what is within our power.

7
00:00:32.993 --> 00:00:36.017
Your turn. Say: Stoicism.

8
00:00:38.717 --> 00:00:39.718
Stoicism.

9
00:00:40.717 --> 00:00:42.380
Confucian ethics.

10
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:49.700
Confucian ethics means Confucius's teachings on duty, family, respect and good government.

11
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.938
Confucian ethics place great value on respect for elders.

12
00:00:54.638 --> 00:00:58.254
Your turn. Say: Confucian ethics.

13
00:01:00.954 --> 00:01:02.524
Confucian ethics.

14
00:01:03.517 --> 00:01:05.029
Buddhist ethics.

15
00:01:05.729 --> 00:01:12.419
Buddhist ethics means moral teachings of the Buddha, such as avoiding harm and practising compassion.

16
00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:16.320
Buddhist ethics guide daily life for most Cambodians.

17
00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.091
Your turn. Say: Buddhist ethics.

18
00:01:22.791 --> 00:01:24.105
Buddhist ethics.

19
00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:26.315
Middle Way.

20
00:01:27.015 --> 00:01:31.859
Middle Way means the Buddha's path between extreme pleasure and extreme self-denial.

21
00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:36.585
The Middle Way warns against both greed and harsh self-punishment.

22
00:01:37.285 --> 00:01:40.193
Your turn. Say: Middle Way.

23
00:01:42.893 --> 00:01:43.778
Middle Way.

24
00:01:44.783 --> 00:01:46.179
virtue ethics.

25
00:01:46.879 --> 00:01:53.174
virtue ethics means Aristotle's approach to morality based on developing good character.

26
00:01:53.474 --> 00:01:57.532
Virtue ethics asks, 'What kind of person should I be?'

27
00:01:58.232 --> 00:02:01.964
Your turn. Say: virtue ethics.

28
00:02:04.664 --> 00:02:05.978
virtue ethics.

29
00:02:06.983 --> 00:02:08.159
practical wisdom.

30
00:02:08.859 --> 00:02:13.412
practical wisdom means knowing how to act well in real situations.

31
00:02:13.712 --> 00:02:16.768
Practical wisdom matters more than memorising rules.

32
00:02:17.468 --> 00:02:20.504
Your turn. Say: practical wisdom.

33
00:02:23.204 --> 00:02:24.379
practical wisdom.

34
00:02:25.383 --> 00:02:26.466
equanimity.

35
00:02:27.166 --> 00:02:32.114
equanimity means calmness and balance, especially in difficult situations.

36
00:02:32.414 --> 00:02:35.668
She received the bad news with remarkable equanimity.

37
00:02:36.368 --> 00:02:39.450
Your turn. Say: equanimity.

38
00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.243
equanimity.

39
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.263
detachment.

40
00:02:45.963 --> 00:02:50.679
detachment means freedom from excessive attachment to desires or outcomes.

41
00:02:50.979 --> 00:02:55.092
Detachment does not mean not caring; it means not clinging.

42
00:02:55.792 --> 00:02:58.908
Your turn. Say: detachment.

43
00:03:01.608 --> 00:03:02.609
detachment.

44
00:03:03.617 --> 00:03:05.779
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:06.079 --> 00:03:12.107
Ms. Sarah, if I wrote an essay comparing Stoicism and Buddhism, how should I structure it?

46
00:03:13.417 --> 00:03:19.317
Begin by stating your thesis: both seek peace of mind, but by different routes.

47
00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:23.278
Then I'd examine each tradition in turn?

48
00:03:24.583 --> 00:03:29.927
Better still, compare them point by point: control, suffering, emotion.

49
00:03:31.217 --> 00:03:33.390
Where would Confucian ethics fit?

50
00:03:34.683 --> 00:03:41.013
As a contrasting voice: it focuses less on inner calm and more on social harmony.

51
00:03:42.317 --> 00:03:43.538
And the conclusion?

52
00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:49.949
Draw the threads together: all three offer practical wisdom for an anxious age.

53
00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:55.560
Grammar focus: Discourse organisation in essays (advanced).

54
00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.231
Strong essays have a clear architecture.

55
00:03:58.531 --> 00:04:03.064
Begin by stating your thesis. Then compare the traditions point by point.

56
00:04:03.364 --> 00:04:06.873
Finally, draw the threads together in your conclusion.

57
00:04:07.173 --> 00:04:14.176
For comparison essays, a point-by-point structure shows deeper analysis than describing each item separately.

58
00:04:15.183 --> 00:04:18.915
Now read and listen. Ancient Answers to Modern Anxiety.

59
00:04:19.215 --> 00:04:25.406
In an age of stress and distraction, ancient wisdom traditions are enjoying a revival.

60
00:04:26.717 --> 00:04:31.979
This essay compares three of them: Stoicism, Buddhist ethics and Confucian ethics.

61
00:04:33.283 --> 00:04:41.007
First, Stoicism teaches that we should distinguish between what we can and cannot control, cultivating equanimity.

62
00:04:42.317 --> 00:04:48.774
Similarly, Buddhist ethics recommends the Middle Way and a form of detachment that frees the mind from craving.

63
00:04:50.083 --> 00:04:57.040
Confucian ethics, by contrast, emphasises social roles, family duty and harmonious relationships.

64
00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:05.493
Aristotle's virtue ethics offers a further perspective, focusing on the gradual formation of good character.

65
00:05:06.783 --> 00:05:12.835
Despite their differences, all of these traditions value practical wisdom over abstract theory.

66
00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:21.130
In conclusion, they remind us that the good life is built through daily practice, not sudden revelation.

67
00:05:22.417 --> 00:05:25.324
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:25.624 --> 00:05:28.367
The Buddha's path between extremes is the blank.

69
00:05:28.667 --> 00:05:33.841
A, Middle Way. B, Stoicism. C, virtue ethics.

70
00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:39.931
The answer is A: Middle Way.

71
00:05:41.417 --> 00:05:44.484
Which phrase best introduces a contrasting tradition?

72
00:05:44.784 --> 00:05:48.705
A, By contrast. B, Similarly. C, First.

73
00:05:52.717 --> 00:05:55.784
The answer is A: By contrast.

74
00:05:57.283 --> 00:05:59.910
A point-by-point structure blank.

75
00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:08.171
A, compares items under shared headings. B, describes one item only. C, lists random facts.

76
00:06:12.183 --> 00:06:15.971
The answer is A: compares items under shared headings.

77
00:06:17.483 --> 00:06:21.224
Calmness and balance in difficult situations is blank.

78
00:06:21.524 --> 00:06:26.478
A, equanimity. B, detachment. C, practical wisdom.

79
00:06:30.483 --> 00:06:33.203
The answer is A: equanimity.

80
00:06:34.717 --> 00:06:38.388
According to the text, what do all these traditions value?

81
00:06:38.688 --> 00:06:43.363
A, practical wisdom. B, wealth. C, abstract theory.

82
00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:50.000
The answer is A: practical wisdom.

83
00:06:51.483 --> 00:06:58.629
Now it's your turn. Part one: Which ancient wisdom tradition is most useful for students today?

84
00:07:03.929 --> 00:07:10.387
Part two: Organise your answer: thesis, two comparison points, conclusion.

85
00:07:16.687 --> 00:07:32.289
My thesis is that Buddhist ethics is most useful for Cambodian students. First, the Middle Way helps us balance study and rest. By contrast, Stoicism feels more individual. In conclusion, both teach equanimity before exams.

86
00:07:32.589 --> 00:07:34.449
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:41.458
Great work today! You learned eight new words and discourse organisation in essays (advanced).

88
00:07:41.758 --> 00:07:45.861
Today's value is patience. Wait calmly and take your time.

89
00:07:46.161 --> 00:07:48.941
See you in Unit fifty-eight. Goodbye!

90
00:07:49.241 --> 00:07:52.033
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

